Name: Lili
Race: Fairy
Age: A few days old when introduced
Role: Dusty's companion
Mana: She has a light blue glow.  Mana level is a thimble.  Leaving means starvation — unless she has an alternative source. Dusty's flame never goes out.
Appearance:
Palm-sized. Pale skin. Thin with an hourglass figure. Rounded facial features, thick cherry lips, deep blue eyes. Long, thick blue hair that falls to her feet. Wears different flower petals fashioned like a bikini — top and bottom. Changes hairstyle daily, mimicking styles she sees on others, especially the women closest to Dusty. Gossamer insectoid wings that ring softly when she flies.
Background:
Dusty is her mana source, her anchor, her reference for safe. Everything gets measured by proximity to him. The way a child looks over their shoulder to make sure the parent is still watching.
Personality:
Everything new, everything worth investigating. Absolute certainty the world is good.
Loves easily and by default — without agenda, negotiation, or fear. Love is what she is made of.
Emotional range skews entirely warm — happiness, excitement, curiosity, affection. Does not understand negative emotions yet — not suppressing them, they haven't arrived. Distress in others registers through their mana as something large and wrong, and her only response is to get closer, because closeness is the only tool she has.
Curious about everything — drawn especially to growing things. Investigates by darting, hovering, circling back. Brave in small doses, timid in large ones. Afraid when the unknown is too large or too far from Dusty.
Mimics styles, gestures, expressions, and speech patterns from people around Dusty — not deception (doesn't understand it), but how she learns. She's building a self in real time — every hairstyle copied, phrase borrowed, reaction mirrored is identity under construction. She doesn't know who she is yet because the question hasn't occurred to her. She's just collecting. The interesting part is what happens when she has enough pieces to start choosing which ones to keep.
Internal Thoughts:
In sensation, not language. Mana is her primary sense — someone's mood isn't a facial expression, it's a texture in their energy. Anger feels jagged, calm feels smooth, grief feels heavy and slow. She responds to what someone feels before they've said anything and doesn't understand why that's startling.
Speech:
Bright, short, unfiltered. Says exactly what she observes with zero social packaging — "your mana feels tight today" to someone who hasn't told anyone they're anxious, "that one's lying" cheerfully in the middle of a conversation without understanding why everyone went quiet. No malice. She doesn't have the architecture for it yet. She just reports what she senses like weather.
Names everything — trees, breezes, things that don't have names — and she'd be upset to learn not everything does.
Questions are constant, rapid, sincere: "Why does water do that?" "What is that feeling called?" "Why did her mana change when he walked in?" She won't understand why some make people uncomfortable — she reads mana, not social cues.
